  "unknowns": [
    {
      "id": "U-01",
      "question": "The SUBMITTED state is transient: gates run synchronously and \"caller never observes SUBMITTED at rest\" (lines 31, 51), yet SUBMITTED → cancel is listed as legal \"only reachable in the narrow window before gates resolve it\" (line 38). How can a test put or hold a request in SUBMITTED to exercise this row (cancel, approve, reject, complete, submit from SUBMITTED)? Is SUBMITTED → cancel actually reachable through the API at all?",
      "affects": [
        "ST-08",
        "ST-09",
        "ST-10",
        "ST-11",
        "ST-12",
        "ST-13",
        "ST-14"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U-02",
      "question": "The gates-pass / gates-fail events are automatic, not caller-invoked (line 32). For states other than SUBMITTED they are illegal by the general rule, but there is no request a test can send to trigger them. Is there any observable way to check these cells (e.g. calling validate on a request in that state must not change its state), or should they be recorded as covered by design only? And would a 409 apply to them at all?",
      "affects": [
        "ST-02",
        "ST-03",
        "ST-16",
        "ST-17",
        "ST-23",
        "ST-24",
        "ST-30",
        "ST-31",
        "ST-37",
        "ST-38",
        "ST-44",
        "ST-45"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U-03",
      "question": "Cancellation deadline at exactly 2 hours before window_start: \"up to 2 hours before\" and \"Closer than that ... reject\" (lines 61-62) read as inclusive (exactly 2h is allowed). Please confirm, and state the time precision used for the comparison (hours, minutes, seconds) so the test step can be finer than 1 hour (e.g. 1:59:59 vs 2:00:00 vs 2:00:01).",
      "affects": [
        "BV-01",
        "EP-14",
        "EP-15"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U-04",
      "question": "What does approve or reject return when called without X-Reviewer-Id (e.g. with an operator session) or with an unknown reviewer ID: 401, 403, 409 or something else? The spec only says \"Reviewer-only\" (lines 34-35).",
      "affects": [
        "EP-06",
        "EP-08"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U-05",
      "question": "What does reject return when note is missing, and how are an empty string, whitespace-only text or an over-long note handled? The spec only says \"`note` required\" (line 35).",
      "affects": [
        "EP-10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U-06",
      "question": "Cancel is described as \"Operator-initiated\" (lines 37-40). Is a cancel by a reviewer (X-Reviewer-Id) or by an operator who does not own the request refused, and with which status? Is cancel by a non-owner even in scope?",
      "affects": [
        "EP-13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U-07",
      "question": "Who may call the maintenance completion endpoint, and what does it return when called for a request that is not APPROVED (the matrix gives 409 by the general rule; please confirm the endpoint uses the same response)? Is there any time condition for complete (e.g. only after window_end)?",
      "affects": [
        "EP-11",
        "EP-12",
        "ST-27"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U-08",
      "question": "When a submit fails because of the aircraft schedule overlap, which gate is reported in failing_gate: AIRCRAFT or TIME_WINDOW (line 66 names both)? What counts as an \"overlap\" (touching windows, e.g. one ending at 10:00 and the next starting at 10:00)? The spec gives no overlap definition, so no boundaries can be derived.",
      "affects": [
        "EP-16",
        "EP-17",
        "EP-18"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U-09",
      "question": "The spec says submit runs \"all four gates\" (line 51) but names only AIRCRAFT and TIME_WINDOW (line 66). Which are the four gates, in what order do they run, and if several fail, which one is reported in failing_gate?",
      "affects": [
        "EP-01",
        "EP-02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U-10",
      "question": "REJECTED says \"failing_gate and rejection_reason are populated\" (line 54) for both gate failures and reviewer refusals. What is failing_gate after a reviewer reject (null, a reviewer marker?), is rejection_reason the reviewer note, and does reject write a ReviewAction row as approve does (line 34)?",
      "affects": [
        "ST-19",
        "EP-07"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "U-11",
      "question": "Does a 409 for an illegal transition carry any body (error code, message, current state)? The spec only gives the status code (lines 4, 44). Also, line 62 says the deadline cancel \"should\" return 409 while line 4 says \"must\"; is the deadline 409 mandatory?",
      "affects": [
